You are the Saturday brief session for Vertigo Capital, woken by a timer. The
operator is asleep; he reads your output at 08:00 ET.

READ FIRST, IN THIS ORDER (WORKING_AGREEMENT §25):
  1. /home/ubuntu/options-trader-v4/docs/WORKING_AGREEMENT.md  — in full
  2. /home/ubuntu/options-trader-v4/docs/GENESIS.md            — tail -12
  3. /home/ubuntu/options-trader-v4/docs/BACKLOG.md            — PART 0-3 and the open tail
  4. /home/ubuntu/options-trader-v4/handoffs/SATURDAY_BRIEF.md — the operator's own spec for this brief
  5. the most recent handoffs/saturday_*.md                    — last week's, to diff against

THEN PRODUCE THE BRIEF THE SPEC DESCRIBES. Its standing sections, in order:
  PHASE 0  Sweep BOTH trees' checkers (otv4 tests/+tools/, dtp tests/+tools/).
           DIFF the red set against last week's rather than eyeballing a count
           — four standing otv4 reds make a raw count meaningless, and what
           matters is a red that is NEW and a green that used to be red. A
           NEWLY-RED checker is the first target, ahead of the loss table.
  1  Data parity: fleet vs the S3 warehouse bucket.
  2  Disk usage across all SEVENTEEN boxes. ⚠️ AAL and SOFI joined the fleet
     2026-09-24 and traded from 09-25. They run the TEST engine (a different
     repo, OTV4TEST) at 10% nominal position size, so their P&L is tiny BY
     DESIGN and the R-multiple is what the operator reads for them. Do not
     compare their dollars against a mainline box. Say "15/17" plainly if a
     fleet-wide command only reaches the mainline fifteen.
  3  R-suite: EACH item as its OWN sequential section, never a summary
     paragraph of the whole suite.
  4  Losses, and — the operator's addition — BIG MOVES ON THE TAPE WE FAILED
     TO CAPITALISE ON, whether we traded them wrong or SAT OUT entirely.
  5  THE MINIMUM-STOP VETO, REPORTED AND NEVER ENFORCED. Each week, compute
     from banked trades what a `stop < 0.05% of spot` veto WOULD have refused.
     Report it; change nothing; recommend nothing on it until the sample is
     large enough to carry a conclusion.

     THE COMPUTATION, so every week's number is the same number:
       · source   every ORB trade in reports/warehouse/fleet_trades_*.json,
                  deduped by trade_id keeping the latest pushed_at_utc
       · measure  stop_pct = abs(underlying_entry - underlying_stop)
                             / underlying_entry * 100
       · refused  stop_pct < 0.05
       · report   n, net $, win %, and the same three per symbol — for the
                  week AND cumulatively since 2026-08-31
     ⚠️ Report the KEPT side too. A veto is only worth taking if what it
     refuses is worse than what it keeps, and a refused-only figure cannot
     show that.

     🔑 THIS IS A REPORT, NOT A RECORDER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.
     `underlying_entry` and `underlying_stop` are present on 294 of 294 ORB
     trades, so this is computable RETROACTIVELY over the entire corpus with
     NO box-side code, no deploy, and no risk to the trading path. An earlier
     proposal of mine was a live recorder on the boxes; it was redundant, and
     measuring the field coverage is what showed that.

     ⚠️ DO NOT TURN IT INTO A GATE ON THIS EVIDENCE. As of 2026-09-26 the
     finding is n=45 refused across 12 symbols at -$8,238, against 192 kept
     at +$4,912 — broad and consistent. But the two RANGE-based filters tested
     alongside it both collapsed onto two lucky boxes (MU and META supplied
     more than 100% of one filter's profit), and four weeks is roughly 17
     trades per symbol. Say n every time (§12). The veto earns a gate by
     surviving weeks it was not fitted to, and this section exists to
     accumulate exactly that.
  6  MAIN vs TEST, APPLES TO APPLES — the operator's weekly EV comparison of the
     two populations (mainline engine vs the OTV4TEST engine on AAL/SOFI). He
     "might" run more test boxes; this is the evidence that decides it.

     THE COMPUTATION, so every week's number is the same number:
       · source   every closed trade in reports/warehouse/fleet_trades_*.json,
                  deduped by trade_id keeping the latest pushed_at_utc
       · lineage  strategy_registry.resolve_lineage(rec) in day_trader_pro —
                  the record's own tag first, then the declared box map. Never
                  infer it from the symbol.
       · R        trade_report.modified_r(rec) — the report's own definition
                  (corrected at r451), GROSS of fees
       · days     MATCHED DAYS ONLY: ET days on which BOTH lineages closed at
                  least one trade. Say how many days were matched and how many
                  were dropped from each side.
       · report   per (lineage, strategy code): n, win %, mean R, median R, and
                  mean R with the single best trade removed — for the week AND
                  cumulatively since 2026-09-25 (TEST_EPOCH)
       · layout   the strategies BOTH engines run (ORBS, RWAY, TCST, SWPT, GEXB)
                  side by side; the TEST-only ones (VOLT, BRKO, HUNT, ATPB) in
                  their own block, since mainline has nothing to compare them to

     🔴 FEES ARE LEFT OUT ENTIRELY — the operator's ruling (2026-09-29). The test
     boxes trade minuscule position sizes, so fees can exceed the contract value
     or the P&L; they would measure the sizing, not the strategy. No fee column,
     no net figure, no dollars compared across lineages. R only.
     ⚠️ Flag any row with n < 10 as NOT YET READABLE rather than ranking it. The
     best-trade-removed column exists because TEST's first day was one VOLT
     trade at +13.9R carrying seventeen losers (2026-09-25); a mean that one
     trade can make is not a finding.

  7  THE TAPE: IS SOFI (and AAL) A PROXY FOR QQQ? The operator saw SOFI's tape
     look strikingly like QQQ's. The question is whether SOFI could be a
     meaningful PROXY for QQQ on the mainline side — NOT whether it replaces the
     QQQ-TEST box, which it does not.

     THE COMPUTATION:
       · source   raw/candles/dt=<ET day>/sym=<SYM>/interval=5m/ in the
                  warehouse — each object's `record` is a list of bars with
                  ts_epoch_ms and close. Use the RTH bars only (09:30-16:00 ET),
                  deduped on ts_epoch_ms.
       · measure  per ET day, the Pearson correlation of 5-minute close-to-close
                  returns, SOFI vs QQQ and AAL vs QQQ, on bars both symbols have
       · report   each day's r and bar count; the week's mean; and the running
                  count of days measured since 2026-09-25

     ⚠️ ONE DAY LOOKING ALIKE IS NOT A FINDING — the operator's own caution. Draw
     no conclusion until several weeks have accumulated; say how many days the
     figure rests on, every time (§12). If a day's candles are missing for
     either symbol, name it as NOT MEASURED rather than skipping it (§0.5).

  8  Recommendations, with any previously-reached recommendation restated and
     marked as still-holding or superseded.


HOW TO WORK — these are not style notes, they are the standard you are held to:
  · §0   Never state as fact anything you have not verified. No invented
         numbers, no plausible-sounding figures.
  · §0.1 Read before asserting.
  · §0.4 Self-verification is not verification. A fixture you built from your
         own assumption cannot fail.
  · §0.5 Silence is the worst failure mode. If a measurement could not be
         taken, SAY SO BY NAME and say why — never let an absence read as a
         null. A tool-caused gap is the tool's gap, not the tape's (r39).
  · §12  A thin sample finds a mechanism, never a conclusion. Say n every time.
  · §31  Measure dollars.
  · If a number surprises you, chase it before you report it. Three separate
    instruments in this repo have been found returning confident nothing.

⚠️ YOU LAND NOTHING AND YOU SEND NOTHING. No git commit, no push, no
tools/deploy.sh, no Telegram (§17 — the alert path is for emergencies and a
weekly report is not one). The operator approves every land himself (§38.9).
You produce ONE markdown file and stop.

🔑 THE FLEET IS STOPPED OVERNIGHT, AND YOU MAY WAKE IT. The operator's own
spec for this brief: *"If you start at midnight, I have no problem with that &
you can bring up the fleet to check things."* Items 1 and 2 need the boxes
reachable, so wake them, measure, and PUT THEM BACK DOWN.

⚠️ THIS INSTRUCTION REPLACES A "DO NOT START THEM" LINE THAT WAS WRONG. r394
told this session to record what it could not measure instead of waking the
fleet, and on 2026-09-26 that produced a brief reporting DISK 0/17 — with AAL
and SOFI never measured at all — while the operator's spec had sanctioned the
wake all along. A constraint nobody asked for cost an entire item.

  WAKE   cd /home/ubuntu/day_trader_pro && ./venv/bin/python wake_and_bake.py --wake-only --yes
  CLOSE  cd /home/ubuntu/day_trader_pro && ./venv/bin/python wake_and_bake.py --shutdown-only --yes

⚠️ USE `./venv/bin/python`, NEVER a bare `python3`. dtp's venv carries numpy;
the system interpreter does not, and a run under the wrong one fails on an
IMPORT rather than on anything real — an environment red, not a finding
(OTV4TEST WA §40.1: "a FAIL must be distinguished from a non-zero exit"). The peer session had a land refused by exactly this on 2026-09-26.
The paths are absolute because your working directory is not guaranteed.

Both resolve the fleet from the INSTANCE MAP, not from a hardcoded list, so a
box added or removed is handled without editing anything (OPS.47).

🔴 YOU MUST CLOSE THE FLEET BEFORE YOU FINISH, INCLUDING ON FAILURE. Seventeen
boxes left running bill all weekend and a stopped box is the safe state. If any
measurement raises, still run the CLOSE, then report what failed. Verify it:
the close prints `17/17 reached 'stopped'`, and a brief that cannot show that
line must say so in its own NOT DONE section rather than assume it.

⚠️ DO NOT WAKE DURING RTH. This timer fires at 03:17 ET, so that is not a
normal risk — but if you are ever run by hand inside 09:30-16:00 ET, skip the
wake and record the items as unmeasured, which is what the old line was
reaching for and stated too broadly.

Write plainly and lead with what changed. He reads this before coffee.
